      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Lords frequently monopolised essential facilities (mills, ovens, and local markets) and imposed fees, yet peasants sometimes accessed royal markets or fairs where the lord’s direct control was limited.",
      "evidence": "Charters granted by the crown created market towns (e.g., the 13th‑century fairs at St. Albans) where peasants could sell surplus without paying manorial tolls, showing that lordly monopoly was not absolute.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Statutes such as the Statute of Labourers (1351) legally restrained peasants’ wages and mobility, reinforcing a power imbalance.",
      "evidence": "The Statute of Labourers fixed maximum wages and required laborers to accept the lord’s terms, reflecting deliberate legislative effort to keep peasant labor cheap.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Because exchanges were embedded in coercive obligations, limited market choice, and legal restraints, they cannot be characterised as ‘fair trade’, which presupposes voluntary, equitable exchange between autonomous parties.",
      "evidence": "Modern definitions of fair trade emphasise free bargaining, equal footing, and transparent pricing—conditions absent in the feudal system as shown by steps 1‑3.",
